<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.1.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00853</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.1.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b256677f-9711-4a64-a198-8e5c78cbb859</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para dir="ltr">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For questions 1 - 4, use the following scenario.
</para>
<blockquote>The relationship between the amount of time a car is parked, in hours, and the cost of parking, in dollars, can be described with a function.</blockquote>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="6df121e7-51aa-4230-bf3e-33db6d202cd7" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e3163c09-c634-4311-89e2-ed8c7dcc3542" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Amount of time car is parked" data-solution-options="[&quot;Amount of time car is parked&quot;, &quot;Cost of parking&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Identify the independent variable in this function.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The independent variable is the amount of time the car is parked.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.1.1 – 4.1.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <exercise data-content-id="be076c59-d230-4f6e-a5a8-03de829c3408" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Cost of parking" data-solution-options="[&quot;Amount of time car is parked&quot;, &quot;Cost of parking&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Identify the dependent variable in this function.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The dependent variable is the cost of parking.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.1.1 – 4.1.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="bfc1ddc2-5d64-452b-8cdb-f5d438a665d5" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="The cost of parking is a function of the amount of time a car is parked." data-solution-options="[&quot;The amount of time a car is parked is a function of the cost of parking.&quot;, &quot;The cost of parking is a function of the amount of time a car is parked.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Correctly describe the function with a sentence of the form “___ is a function of ___ .”</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The cost of parking is a function of the amount of time a car is parked.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.1.1-4.1.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>
<!-- New pset with 3 attemps -->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="f454252c-7da7-438a-8dd4-be91cfba4052" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q1bb-->
  <exercise data-content-id="90dae51d-bab9-48b6-8f3f-2591b15904e4" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="2 hours of parking costs a total of $6" data-solution-options="[&quot;2 hours of parking costs $6 per hour&quot;, &quot;6 hours of parking costs $2 per hour&quot;, &quot;2 hours of parking costs a total of $6&quot;, &quot;6 hours of parking costs a total of $2&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Suppose it costs $3 per hour to park, with a maximum cost of $12. Here is a graph of the situation.</para>
        <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/25e5503db717b9e7113c3af27bc4b60c4bb4b73a"/></media></figure><para>What does the point \((2,6)\) mean in this situation?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 2 hours of parking costs a total of $6.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.1.1 – 4.1.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ac4763ea-4ee2-457d-9d97-810c889f21cf" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;Time since the person starts walking is the input.&quot;, &quot;\\(t\\) represents the input.&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;Distance is the input.&quot;, &quot;Time of day is the input.&quot;, &quot;Time since the person starts walking is the input.&quot;, &quot;\\(t\\) represents the input.&quot;, &quot;\\(d\\) represents the input.&quot;, &quot;The input is not measured in any particular unit.&quot;, &quot;The input is measured in hours.&quot;, &quot;For each input, there are sometimes two outputs.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The distance a person walks, \(d\), in kilometers, is a function of time, \(t\), in minutes, since the walk began.<emphasis>two statements</emphasis> about the input variable of this function that are true.
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Time since the person started walking is the input; \(t\) represents the input.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.1.3 – 4.1.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a9b84d60-438d-4247-acdd-690abbfaa3a6" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Determine if the following relation is a function.
          </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! No, For the following reasons:</para>
      <list>
        <item>Since the input value 1 goes to both –1 and 1, this relation is not a function.</item>
        <item>Since the input value 27 goes to both -3 and 3, this relation is not a function. </item>
        <item>Since the input value 8 goes to both -2 and 2, this relation is not a function.</item>
      </list>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.1.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="57e4d49e-9060-4dfc-a5b8-34c9a88877ab" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Is the following relation a function? Explain your answer.<figure><media alt="This figure shows two table that each have one column. The table on the left has the header “Name” and lists the names “Neal”, “Krystal”, “Kelvin”, “George”, “Christa”, and “Mike”. The table on the right has the header “Phone number” and lists the numbers “123-567-4389 work”, “231-378-5941 cell”, “753-469-9731 cell”, “567-534-2970 work”, “684-369-7231 cell”, “798-367-8541 cell”, and “639-847-6971 cell”. There are arrows that start at a name and points toward a number in the phone number table. The first arrow goes from Neal to 753-469-9731 cell. The second arrow goes from Krystal to a 684-369-7231 cell. The third arrow goes from Kelvin to 231-378-5941 cell. The fourth arrow goes from George to 123-567-4389 work. The fifth arrow goes from George to 639-847-6971 cell. The sixth arrow goes from Christa to 567-534-2970 work. The seventh arrow goes from Mike to 567-534-2970 work. The eighth arrow goes from Mike to 798-367-8541 cell."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ba119f3829ed1c6ac115b2828bcd349040f3e4e8"/></media></figure></para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! No, this relation is not a function. Since both George and Mike are mapped to two different phone numbers, every input does not have exactly one output value.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.1.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="76cceca0-7dcb-4087-9dd4-4feff6f13cb3" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Determine if the following relation is a function.
          </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! This relation is a function. Every input has exactly one output value</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.1.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
